# Prisma CLI env (used by `prisma generate`/`migrate`/`studio` run from this package).
# All 3 apps (portalAdmin, portalPartner, rentoCustomer) point at this SAME database —
# copy the values from any one of their .env.local files, they're identical.

# Pooled connection (Neon's pgbouncer, "-pooler" host) — used for normal query traffic.
DATABASE_URL=postgresql://user:password@ep-example-pooler.region.aws.neon.tech/neondb?sslmode=require

# Direct, non-pooled connection (no "-pooler") — required by `prisma migrate` since
# migrations need a session-mode connection that pgbouncer's transaction mode can't give.
DIRECT_URL=postgresql://user:password@ep-example.region.aws.neon.tech/neondb?sslmode=require

# Optional shared cache (see src/cache.ts) — an Upstash Redis REST endpoint. Entirely
# optional: every app runs correctly with these unset, it just hits Neon on every read
# instead of serving cached vehicle listings/ratings from Redis first. If you DO set
# these, use the SAME Upstash database's credentials in every one of the 3 apps'
# .env.local (portalAdmin, portalPartner, rentoCustomer) AND here — that's what lets a
# write in one app (e.g. portalPartner adding a vehicle) instantly invalidate a cache
# entry a different app (rentoCustomer) is serving from, instead of each app only ever
# clearing its own copy.
#
# Get these from your Upstash console (upstash.com) → your Redis database → REST API
# section. If you provisioned Redis via Vercel's "Marketplace Database Providers" (which
# is Upstash under the hood) instead, its integration sets KV_REST_API_URL/TOKEN — those
# are read as a fallback, so no extra config is needed in that case.
UPSTASH_REDIS_REST_URL=https://example-12345.upstash.io
UPSTASH_REDIS_REST_TOKEN=<paste the REST token here>

